      <EssayHeader slug="the-org-chart-travels" />

      <p class="e-thesis"><span class="e-thesis-label">In short:</span> When American ideas about identity and workplace culture spread abroad, what travels is not the argument but the organizational machinery (policies, training modules, forms) that multinational companies and platforms install for administrative convenience. That machinery works without anyone being persuaded, which explains the resentment it provokes.</p>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">A regional office outside Kuala Lumpur runs a ninety-minute module on unconscious bias. The slides were built in California. They have been “localized,” which means the stock photos were changed and the text was translated. They still contain a case study about a hiring panel, a candidate with a name the local staff cannot place, and a legal framework that does not exist in Malaysia. Everyone completes it. Everyone is bored. Nobody, anywhere in the chain between the vendor and the room, was asked whether the thing being described is a problem in that country.</p>
        <p>This is what the export of an ideology actually looks like. Getting it right matters, because almost every argument about whether this phenomenon is “global” assumes that ideas travel the way ideas are supposed to travel: by persuasion, in the local language, contested by the people who live there. That is not the mechanism. The mechanism is procurement.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="my-14">
        <img
          src="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-04-193850/arctic-60n-0e/preview-1200.webp"
          width="1024"
          height="1024"
          alt="Psychedelic poster painting of Earth as a glowing orb encircled by neon rainbow rings in a starfield"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto t-plate"
        />
        <figcaption class="e-cap mt-3">
          60N 0E, Arctic, rendered through a Peter Max lens from that day’s atmospheric data.
          <a href="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-04-193850/arctic-60n-0e/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="underline hover:opacity-70">View the painting</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Word Is Not the Thing</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Start by separating three things that are habitually confused: a word, a set of underlying disputes, and a machine for distributing policy.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The word is “woke,” and it is American and carries American history. It comes from Black vernacular speech, an admonition to stay alert to racial injustice, traceable in recordings to the 1930s. It was revived through a song in 2008 and through the protests that followed Ferguson in 2014. Around 2019 its opponents captured it and turned it into an insult, which is the ordinary fate of any term that becomes useful to the other side.</p>
        <p>The result is that outside America the word works almost entirely as a weapon. Nobody in Lyon or Seoul describes themselves as woke. They are described that way, usually by a politician, usually in a sentence about foreign contamination. So the question “does wokeness exist in country X?” has a trivial answer about vocabulary and a serious answer about substance, and most conversations switch the two.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Apparatus Is the Payload</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The serious answer begins with what actually crosses a border reliably. It is not an argument. It is an organizational chart.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A multinational adopts a policy at headquarters. It spreads to subsidiaries because consistency is easier to administer than variation, and because lawyers prefer one global standard to forty local ones.</li>
          <li>A vendor is hired, and the vendor sells the same product in every market, because that is the only way its economics work.</li>
          <li>A university builds an administrative office modeled on an American one, because the American one is the prestige template and its job descriptions are available to copy.</li>
          <li>A platform writes moderation rules in one country and applies them in a hundred and ninety.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-6">By the time any of this reaches a person, the idea has not arrived as a claim to be evaluated. It has arrived as a mandatory field in a system, a required module before the quarter closes, a policy in the handbook with an effective date. It has already won, procedurally, without ever being argued in the country where it landed.</p>
        <p>That mechanism explains most of what people find infuriating about the phenomenon abroad, and it does so without anyone having to agree about the merits. Even someone entirely sympathetic to the underlying claim is entitled to notice that it was never put to them. Compliance is not persuasion. It replaces a debate with an org chart, and it produces the particular resentment people feel toward things that are moralizing and non-negotiable at the same time.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Apparatus Had a Theorist</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">None of this is new, and the priority is embarrassing enough to state plainly. In 1970 a French Marxist philosopher, Louis Althusser, published an essay in <em>La Pensée</em> called “<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ideology_and_Ideological_State_Apparatuses" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:opacity-70 t-amber">Ideology and Ideological State Apparatuses</a>.” The section above is largely his.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Althusser distinguished two kinds of apparatus. The <em>repressive</em> apparatus is the state’s capacity for force: government, courts, police and army. It is unified and coercive, and it holds ideology in reserve behind the violence. The <em>ideological</em> apparatuses run the other way round, with ideology first and the coercion hidden and symbolic. He listed them as religious, educational, family, legal, political, trade-union, communications and cultural. His central and genuinely strange claim was that ideology does not live in anybody’s head. It has a material existence. It exists in practices, in rituals, in the apparatus itself. In Kuala Lumpur, that means not what the staff believe about the module, but the module.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The mechanism he proposed for how it takes hold is “interpellation.” Picture a policeman in the street shouting “Hey, you there!” The person turns around, and in turning becomes a subject: someone addressed, someone the call was meant for. The turn is the entire operation. Belief is not required and was never requested. Only recognition is required.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">This accounts for the detail in Kuala Lumpur that otherwise looks like the apparatus failing. Everyone is bored, and everyone completes it. But boredom is not resistance and completion is not assent, and the system asked for neither. The employee clicking “acknowledge” while privately mocking the slides has turned around. What the module wanted was a timestamp in a database, retrievable by lawyers in a jurisdiction half a day away. It got exactly that from the skeptic and the believer alike, which is why nobody administering it has ever cared which is which.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Now two amendments, because Althusser’s essay has aged in an interesting direction.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>The employer has replaced the school.</strong> Althusser held that the school had taken over from the church as the dominant ideological apparatus. On current evidence the employer has taken over from the school. An adult spends more waking hours inside it, it grades continuously rather than annually, and its penalties are material in a way a bad mark never was.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>The apparatus no longer belongs to a state.</strong> The deeper amendment concerns the middle word of his term. Every apparatus on his list is national. Church, school, family, courts, parties and press are each bounded by a state, staffed by its citizens, and arguing in its language. He called them <em>state</em> apparatuses because in 1970 an apparatus and a state were the same size, and that had been true for long enough to look like a law of nature. It is not true now. The dominant apparatus today has no state. It has a headquarters, a general counsel, a vendor contract, and operations in a hundred and ninety jurisdictions. It can install a practice in Malaysia without the Malaysian state having any view on the matter, or being asked, or in most cases finding out. Drop the word “state,” and what is left is an ideological apparatus of a kind that did not exist before. It is the first to reproduce a set of social relations across societies rather than within one.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">That difference is the whole source of the resentment described above. Being shaped by your own society’s apparatus is invisible from the inside. It feels like weather, or common sense, or nothing at all, which is the condition Althusser wanted his readers to notice. Being shaped by somebody else’s apparatus is not invisible in the least. It arrives with a foreign case study attached, and it feels like an instruction, because that is exactly what it is. The imported version fails at the one thing the domestic version does well, which is to disappear.</p>
        <p>Two cautions, and the second is the serious one. Althusser was a Marxist working out why the revolution had not arrived, and nothing here is that argument. More importantly, his framework has a defect its admirers rarely admit. If everything is ideology and the call always lands, then no observation can count against the theory, and the analysis ends in a shrug. “Ideology has no history,” he wrote, and is eternal. A claim that cannot be wrong has a serious flaw, not a strength. The mechanism is worth keeping. The fatalism is not, and there is a cheap way to test the difference, which I describe in “The Test” below.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Local Fights Have Local Content</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">There are real disputes in these countries, and they run on domestic material that the imported framework describes badly.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>France</strong> is the sharpest case, because it has a rival universalism of its own. Republican citizenship and <em>laïcité</em> (strict state secularism) are not a hedge against identity politics. They are an explicit alternative to it, and the state does not collect ethnic statistics, which makes American racial categories close to unusable as an administrative matter. In 2018 the National Assembly voted to strike the word “race” from the first article of the constitution, although the revision was never completed. Ministers have attacked what they call <em>wokisme</em> as an American import threatening the republican model, and inclusive writing has been restricted in official documents and schools. Whatever one thinks of that, it is not a country failing to have the conversation. It is a country that had a different conversation first and reached a different answer.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>Germany</strong> runs into its own memory culture. Holocaust remembrance is institutionalized to a degree with no American parallel. Imported anti-colonial frameworks have collided with it directly and publicly, in the controversy around a Cameroonian theorist in 2020 and in the antisemitism scandal that engulfed a major art exhibition in 2022. The result is not a German version of the American argument. It is a different argument, in which two moral vocabularies with strong local claims are in conflict.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>Canada and Australia</strong> organize around settler and Indigenous relations rather than the American racial binary. Australia’s 2023 referendum on an Indigenous Voice to Parliament failed by roughly sixty to forty, and it carried no state.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>India</strong> has run identity politics of enormous consequence since well before America exported anything, structured around caste and religion, with a reservation (quota) policy older than most of the vocabulary now being imported onto it.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>South Korea’s</strong> central conflict is gender, conducted in entirely domestic slang, and it has swung national elections.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>Latin America</strong> generated mass feminist movements on its own, and then its own noisy reaction against them.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>Chinese internet users</strong> coined their own epithet for Western progressives, <em>baizuo</em> (“white left”), with no help from anybody.</p>
        <p>The pattern is consistent. Where the conflict is real it is local, and where the vocabulary is American, the vocabulary is usually the least interesting part of it.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Legal Substrate Explains the Difference in Style</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">One structural fact accounts for more cross-country variation than any amount of cultural analysis, and it is almost always left out.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The United States has a constitutional prohibition on regulating speech. Germany, France and the United Kingdom do not, and they have working hate speech statutes, press regulation and public order law. So the same impulse to police expression that runs through a statute in Europe has nowhere official to go in America. It flows instead into employment, platform policy, professional association rules and social punishment.</p>
        <p>American cancel culture is therefore not evidence of a uniquely censorious population. It is what censoriousness looks like in a system that has closed the legislative route. The European version is calmer in the way a regulated utility is calmer than an unregulated market, and just as final. A German prosecuted for a post and an American fired for one have both been sanctioned for speech. Only the German can appeal to a court, which is a point in Europe’s favor. Only the German is subject to a rule enacted by a legislature that can be voted out, which is also a point in Europe’s favor. The American arrangement gets the worst of both: no due process and no democratic authorship.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Backlash Is an Export Too</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The tidy story, in which an American ideology spreads outward while local traditions resist, is wrong in one important way. The counter-movement is at least as international and at least as professionalized. This follows directly from the section above: an apparatus is indifferent to what it carries, so anyone who enjoyed this analysis while it was aimed at a compliance department should expect it to work identically when pointed the other way.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>An Argentine president denounces <em>wokismo</em> from a stage in Davos, in the vocabulary of an American think tank.</li>
          <li>A Hungarian government builds an entire electoral identity around “gender ideology” as a foreign imposition.</li>
          <li>Russia moves from a law against “propaganda” to designating an international movement as extremist, and markets the whole package abroad as evidence of Western decadence.</li>
          <li>A Ugandan legislature passes severe legislation and frames rights advocacy as colonialism in modern dress. That is effective rhetoric precisely because the earlier export really was clumsy and really did arrive as a condition attached to aid.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>That last point deserves more discomfort than it usually gets from either side. The manner of the export handed genuinely repressive governments an argument that sounds defensible. The people who pay for that are local and vulnerable, and they are now less protected than before. Delivering universal claims as compliance does not merely fail to persuade. It discredits the claims themselves, and does so most severely in the places with the least margin for error.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Thin Version and the Thick Version</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">That leaves the question worth answering: how much of this is universal?</p>
        <p class="mb-6">There is a <strong>thin version</strong> that generalizes without difficulty. It is thinner than its advocates like and more substantial than its opponents admit. Do not harass people at work. Hire and promote on the merits, and be able to show your reasoning. Apply rules to everyone. Give someone a hearing before you ruin them. None of that needs an American vocabulary. Most of it predates that vocabulary by centuries, and no country needs an imported module to understand it. It is ordinary liberal proceduralism, and it survives translation because it is a claim about how institutions should operate rather than about how history should be told.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Then there is the <strong>thick version</strong>, which is culturally specific and travels badly: a particular racial taxonomy, a particular national guilt narrative, a particular theory of how identity determines standing in an argument, and a particular set of speech conventions produced by American institutions in a specific decade. Transplanted, these produce category errors rather than progress. Applying American racial categories in a country that legally refuses to collect them is not a bold reform. It is an administrative impossibility. Importing a settler-colonial framework into a society whose central historical wound is a genocide it committed in Europe, as in Germany, produces a collision rather than a synthesis.</p>
        <p>The honest position is that the thin version should be universal because it already nearly is. The thick version should stay home, not because it is wicked but because it is parochial, and parochial things become ridiculous in transit.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Test</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">For anyone running a multinational, here are three questions to ask about any people policy arriving from headquarters.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Was this argued in the country where it will apply, in the local language, by people who live with the consequences, or was it only translated?</li>
          <li>Who is the customer for it? If the honest answer is the parent company’s general counsel, say so in the memo rather than presenting it as a local initiative.</li>
          <li>Most important: if headquarters stopped requiring this tomorrow, would the local organization keep any part of it?</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mb-6">That last question separates real commitment from compliance more cleanly than any survey, and the answer is usually known to everyone in the building before the question is asked. A practice that would not survive the removal of the mandate was never adopted. It was administered.</p>
        <p>It is also the test that Althusser’s theory never supplied. An account in which everyone is always already a subject has no control group, so it can name the apparatus and cannot measure it. A mandate is better than a theory in exactly one respect: it can be switched off. Switch it off in one region for two quarters, tell nobody it is an experiment, and count what remains. Whatever survives the withdrawal of the call was a belief. Everything else was a timestamp.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Mars</h2>
      <figure class="my-14">
        <img
          src="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-04-193850/europe-30n-15w/preview-1200.webp"
          width="1024"
          height="1024"
          alt="Psychedelic poster painting of a magenta canyon landscape under a rainbow arc, with Earth glowing small in the sky"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto t-plate"
        />
        <figcaption class="e-cap mt-3">
          30N 15W, Eastern Atlantic, rendered through a Peter Max lens from that day’s atmospheric data.
          <a href="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-04-193850/europe-30n-15w/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="underline hover:opacity-70">View the painting</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">This pattern will not stop at national borders, because the mechanism has nothing to do with nations. It is a property of organizations that operate in more than one place and prefer one policy to several.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>Policy will arrive from Earth.</strong> The first permanent settlement off Earth will be run by an organization with a headquarters, a general counsel and a global standard. Its personnel policies will be written to satisfy an authority on a different planet. They will arrive in the payload alongside the water reclamation hardware, and they will be adjusted on a schedule set by legal exposure in a jurisdiction that is between three and twenty-two light minutes away and has never seen the place. Nobody on Mars will have been asked. The module will still be mandatory, and it will still contain a case study about a hiring panel.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>The law already allows it.</strong> The legal groundwork for this exists and is more accommodating than people assume. The Outer Space Treaty forbids any nation from claiming a celestial body, so nobody will own the ground. But its eighth article says that the state on whose registry an object is carried keeps jurisdiction and control over that object and any personnel aboard, and a habitat is an object. The ground cannot be claimed and the building can, so the settlement will be governed the way a ship is governed: by the flag it is registered under, not by the water it sits in. Jurisdiction arrives as cargo. That is not a loophole. It is the only workable answer anyone has found to the question of which law applies where no law was in force. It also guarantees that every apparatus described in this essay lands intact, with a warrant, at the destination.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>A private version has already been drafted.</strong> This is the part that should hold your attention. The Starlink terms of service, a consumer broadband contract, have for years carried a clause about services on Mars or in transit to it. It says the parties recognise Mars as a free planet, that no Earth-based government has authority or sovereignty over Martian activities, and that disputes will be settled through self-governing principles established in good faith at the time of settlement. Read that as what it is. It is not a constitution. It is a choice-of-law clause, sitting in the same document that governs a billing dispute about a dish on a roof in Nebraska. It does not create a government so much as decline to name one. “Self-governing principles to be established in good faith” is an unsigned promissory note, drawn on a future in which the promisor will also be the employer, the landlord, the utility, the medical provider and the atmosphere. The settlers will have accepted the document that disclaims sovereignty over their settlement by clicking through it.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>The light lag makes the mechanism worse, not better.</strong> Mars is between three and twenty-two minutes from Earth at the speed of light, depending on where the two planets are, so a question and its answer cannot happen within the same hour. For anything requiring judgment, that is a real constraint on central control: nobody at headquarters can help with a seal failure, and everybody knows it. But recall what the apparatus actually wanted. Not a conversation, not agreement, not even attention. A timestamp in a database, retrievable by lawyers. Interpellation has no return leg. The policeman shouts, the subject turns, and the turning is the whole operation. So it survives a delay of twenty-two minutes exactly as well as a delay of none, because nobody was waiting for a reply. Every part of the relationship that needs a round trip will pass to the settlement within a decade. The one part that does not need a round trip is the part that will not.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>So the division of authority is predictable, and close to the opposite of what a settler would choose.</strong> Discretion will be delegated wherever delay is lethal: the abort call, the surgery, the decision to vent a module. It will not be delegated over policy, because policy is the cheapest thing in the payload and the most expensive thing to be caught without. Training content weighs nothing, travels at light speed, costs a vendor license, and its absence is the first fact a plaintiff’s lawyer will establish. Under those economics no general counsel will ever propose that the module stay home. The settlement will be trusted with the hull and not with the handbook. The people living inside the first structure in history where the atmosphere is a management decision will find that the thing headquarters held onto most tightly was the paperwork.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>The test stops working here.</strong> I should concede that plainly. “Switch the mandate off in one region for two quarters and count what survives” needs a second region. Mars will be one settlement, of a few hundred people, under one operator, for a long time. There will be no control group, no comparison population, and no chance of a quiet natural experiment, because everyone would notice, and because the operator would be running the experiment on the only sample that exists. The tool that separates a belief from a timestamp is unavailable at exactly the place where the distinction matters most. It will stay unavailable until there is a second settlement with a different flag on its registry, which is an argument for competition in a place where competition sounds like a luxury.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>The thin and thick versions separate more sharply out there, not less.</strong> The thin version becomes more necessary than it has ever been on Earth: apply the rules to everyone, give a person a hearing before you ruin them, be able to show your reasoning. In a workplace with no courts, no legislature, no press and no exit, ordinary proceduralism is not a nicety imported from a law school. It is the entire stock of protection available to a human being, and it should be written down early by people who know they are writing a constitution and not a policy. The thick version becomes farce. A taxonomy built from the specific history of one continent of one planet is delivered to a room where nobody has seen either. It comes in a case study about a hiring panel that met in a city six to nine months of travel away. That is parochial in the exact sense this essay has been using: not wicked, just from somewhere, and visibly so once the somewhere is far enough away to see whole. The demographic field will still be on the form. It will still carry a jurisdiction in its label. It will just have run out of jurisdictions to name.</p>
        <p>That is not a prediction about ideology. It is a prediction about org charts, which are the only thing in this entire argument with a proven ability to cross a vacuum.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-14 pt-8 border-t text-[13px] leading-[1.75]" style="border-color:var(--t-rule); color:var(--t-muted);">
        A note on sources: the Althusser reading is owed to my good friend August Gold. He read this essay and pointed out that a fair portion of it was re-deriving, at some length, a paper published in 1970. The section “The Apparatus Had a Theorist” exists because he was right, and the argument is sturdier for having an older and more dangerous author standing behind it.
      </p>

